# loopback6.poke -- the same conversation as loopback.poke, over IPv6.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS, and it is not symmetry. AF_INET6 is 28 on FreeBSD and 10 on
# Linux. That divergence is the HEADLINE EXAMPLE in the tier 10 table and in
# CONVENTIONS section 3 -- the case the whole "nothing whose numeric value is
# chosen by an OS header may appear in a frame" rule was written around -- and
# until this file there was no fixture anywhere that sent domain 2. The ABI
# named IPv6 in section 7.5, the seam mapped it, and nothing ever asked.
#
# So the rule with the most prose behind it had the least evidence under it.
# A fixture that sends domain 1 proves the mapping works for the family whose
# number HAPPENS TO AGREE across the two platforms, which is exactly the case
# that cannot fail.
#
# WHAT IT ACTUALLY CHECKS, since "IPv6 works" is not a claim a test can make.
# The domain byte on the wire is 02 on both platforms, because 2 is
# brainstem's own number for IPv6 and not the kernel's. The frames this
# program emits are therefore byte-identical on FreeBSD and Linux, and tier 10
# compares them as such. If the seam ever leaked AF_INET6 onto the wire the
# two guests would disagree by 28 against 10 and the comparison would say so.
# That is the property; connecting to oneself is merely how it is provoked.
#
# THIS FIXTURE NEEDS NO SECOND PROCESS, for the reason loopback.poke gives at
# length: bind replies with the address actually bound, so the program reads
# its own ephemeral port out of that reply and emits it back in the connect
# frame. No helper binary, no agreed port, nothing left running.
#
# THE PORT IS CARRIED IN RAW BRAINFUCK, because it is a value and not a
# literal, and bfgen only ever writes literals. Cell 0 is the working cell
# every EMIT and READ uses; the two raw blocks below park the port in cells 1
# and 2 and later read them out.
#
# THE ADDRESS IS ::1, SIXTEEN OCTETS IN READING ORDER -- fifteen zero bytes
# and then 01 -- at offsets 4 to 19 of the address record, with twelve zero
# bytes of padding after it. The record is 32 bytes for EVERY family, so the
# bind and connect frames are the same length here as in loopback.poke and
# only their contents differ. Those zeros cost nothing to emit: '.' on a cell
# never touched.
#
# Frames transcribed by hand from ABI.md sections 3, 6, 7.5 to 7.9 and 7.10.
#
#   hello  op 01, len 10.  This program touches no filesystem at all; the
#   three handles it is given back are the broker's own stdio and it
#   ignores them.
EMIT 01 0a 00 42 53 54 4d 01 00 00 00 00 00
READ 107
#   socket  op 05, domain 2 IPv6, type 1 stream, protocol 0.  Handle 4 --
#   handles 1, 2 and 3 are always the broker's stdin, stdout and stderr.
EMIT 05 03 00 02 01 00
READ 7
#   bind  op 07, handle 4, flags 1 REUSEADDR, then the 32 byte address:
#   family 02, reserved 00, port 0000 meaning "any", then ::1 in READING
#   ORDER and twelve zero bytes.
EMIT 07 26 00 04 00 00 00 01 00
EMIT 02 00 00 00
EMIT 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00
EMIT 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 01
EMIT 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00
#   the reply is status, two length bytes, then the address bound.  Drain the
#   status, the length, the family and the reserved byte: five bytes.
READ 5
#   now the two port bytes, into cells 1 and 2, little endian as they lie
>,>,<<
#   and the remaining twenty eight bytes of the address record
READ 28
#   listen  op 08, handle 4, backlog 0 meaning 128
EMIT 08 06 00 04 00 00 00 00 00
READ 3
#   socket  op 05 again, domain 2.  Handle 5, the client.
EMIT 05 03 00 02 01 00
READ 7
#   connect  op 06, handle 5, flags 0, family 02, reserved 00 ...
EMIT 06 26 00 05 00 00 00 00 00 02 00
#   ... then the port this program was given, straight out of cells 1 and 2 ...
>.>.<<
#   ... then ::1 and twelve zeros.
EMIT 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00
EMIT 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 01
EMIT 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00
READ 3
#   accept  op 09, handle 4, flags 0.  The reply is a handle and the peer
#   address: 3 header + 4 + 32 = 39 bytes.  Handle 6.
EMIT 09 06 00 04 00 00 00 00 00
READ 39
#   write  op 0b, handle 5 (the client end), flags 0, "v6".  Two bytes that
#   are not loopback.poke's "hi", so a trace tells the two fixtures apart.
EMIT 0b 08 00 05 00 00 00 00 00 76 36
READ 5
#   read  op 0a, handle 6 (the accepted end), ONE BYTE AT A TIME.  A read
#   returns UP TO n bytes and a stream may deliver them in any grouping it
#   likes, so asking for two and assuming two is a race waiting for a busier
#   machine.  See drive.poke, where exactly that happened.
EMIT 0a 08 00 06 00 00 00 01 00 00 00
READ 4
EMIT 0a 08 00 06 00 00 00 01 00 00 00
READ 4
#   close all three, youngest first
EMIT 0c 04 00 06 00 00 00
READ 3
EMIT 0c 04 00 05 00 00 00
READ 3
EMIT 0c 04 00 04 00 00 00
READ 3
#   exit  op 02, len 1, code 0
EMIT 02 01 00 00
READ 3
